# 🍬 LeetCode 135: Candy (Slope / Peak & Valley Pattern)

| Attribute | Details |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Difficulty** | Hard |
| **Pattern** | Greedy (Slope / Peak & Valley Traversal) |
| **Time Complexity** | $\mathcal{O}(N)$ — Single linear pass |
| **Space Complexity** | $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Zero extra memory allocated |

---

> [!NOTE]
> **Why do we need the $O(1)$ Slope Approach?**
> The standard Two-Pass Greedy approach requires an extra $O(N)$ vector to store candy counts. By visualizing the ratings array as a continuous **mountain landscape** (up-slopes, down-slopes, and flat plateaus), we can count the exact total candies on the fly in a single pass without storing anything in memory.

---

## 🧠 The Core Concept & Intuition

Think of the children's ratings as elevations on a hiking trail:

```text
               Peak (Peak Height = 4)
                /\
               /  \
              /    \
  (Peak = 2) /      \
            /\       \  Flat Plateau (Reset to 1)
           /  \       \________
__________/    \       
 Up-Slope      Down-Slope (Valley)

#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

class Solution {
public:
    int candy(std::vector<int>& ratings) {
        const int n = ratings.size();
        if (n <= 1) return n;

        int sum = 1; // First child gets at least 1 candy
        int i = 1;

        while (i < n) {
            // Case 1: Flat Plateau (Equal ratings)
            if (ratings[i] == ratings[i - 1]) {
                sum += 1;
                i++;
                continue;
            }

            // Case 2: Up-Slope (Uphill climb)
            int peak = 1;
            while (i < n && ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) {
                peak++;
                sum += peak;
                i++;
            }

            // Case 3: Down-Slope (Downhill descent)
            int down = 1;
            while (i < n && ratings[i] < ratings[i - 1]) {
                sum += down;
                down++;
                i++;
            }

            // Peak Correction: If the downhill descent is longer than the uphill climb
            if (down > peak) {
                sum += (down - peak);
            }
        }

        return sum;
    }
};

